<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erzberg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Styrian Erzberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Erzberg is a mountain near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenerz">Eisenerz</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, made largely of siderite iron ore and mined since at least the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Open-cast mining has cut it into giant terraces, giving it the look of a stepped pyramid. Its iron made Styria one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s main iron-producing regions, and it is still worked today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erzberg.jpg" alt="Erzberg" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerald Senarclens de Grancy · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erzberg3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Erzberg lies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenerz">Eisenerz</a> in the central-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, 60 km north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> and 260 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. It is the largest iron ore deposit in Austria, with estimated reserves of 235 million tonnes of ore. The deposit lies at the northern fringe of the eastern greywacke zone, a band of Paleozoic metamorphosed sedimentary rocks that runs east to west through the Austrian Alps. Most of the siderite is hosted in Silurian and Devonian marbles of the Noric nappe, and the ore, made up of siderite, ankerite and ferrous dolomite, holds on average 21% iron.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Iron has been mined at the Erzberg for more than 1,300 years. A document once held by the Oswaldikirche, the Catholic church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenerz">Eisenerz</a>, dated the start of mining to 712, although this is unconfirmed. At first the ore was dug at the surface: limonite found close to the surface was mined in the 12th century under exploitation rights granted by territorial rulers, and it was smelted in small forges near the pits, where the miners lived at little more than subsistence.</p><p>Underground mining with hammer and pick began in the 16th century on the orders of the Austrian emperor. The upper part of the mountain was worked by the townsfolk of Vordernberg and the lower part by the people of Innerberg, the former name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenerz">Eisenerz</a>. Forges known as Radwerke, their bellows driven by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-wheel">water wheels</a>, were built in the valleys of Eisenerz and Vordernberg and drew further settlement. Ore was at first carried down to them by hand on sleighs, the Sackzug, with women assisting, before horses and carts came into use, and from 1810 to 1870 the shafts and tunnels were fitted with rails and miners' carts.</p><p>Black powder was tried on the mountain in 1720 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dynamite">dynamite</a> in 1870, and from 1876 electrical cables connected the blasting caps. From 1906 the miners used carbide acetylene lamps and percussion drills driven by compressed air. Underground mining gave way to open-pit terraces from 1890. Mine trucks appeared in 1951 and had replaced the carts and rails by 1963, and later automation and remote control of the work cut the workforce sharply.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The mine produces around 3.2 million tonnes of iron ore a year, which is shipped to Voestalpine AG in Linz and south to the smelter at Donawitz. Its old tunnels can be visited by a tourist train, and the site is the scene of the annual Erzberg Rodeo, a hard enduro motorbike race.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Erzberg mine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Iron from the <b>Erzberg</b> made it an industrial heartland.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-styria">Duchy of Styria</a></li><li><blockquote>Iron mining at the <b>Erzberg</b> near Eisenerz became a lasting source of wealth.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-styria">Margraviate of Styria</a></li></ul></section>
